# rules: use-of-weak-rsa-key
# languages: kotlin
# fixture: kotlin/CWE-326.kt
# from: kotlin/lang/security/weak-rsa.yaml
#
# NIST has said 2048 bits since 2015 and 1024-bit RSA has been within reach of
# a well-funded attacker for longer than that. A key that is too short does not
# fail; it works exactly as well as a long one right up until somebody factors
# the modulus, and then every signature it ever made is forgeable.
#
# As translated the rule was written around `$KEY = $G.getInstance("RSA")`, an
# assignment, which is not how a Kotlin program introduces a value - `val` and
# `var` make a declaration, a different node, and the rule matched nothing on
# any Kotlin file ever written. Both spellings are here now.
#
# The two statements are matched together and the two holes compared, because
# the generator that was asked for RSA has to be the generator that was given
# the size. Written as one hole twice it would mean the same thing and leave
# only one span to report at, and the size is the half worth pointing at.
#
# `where_capture_compare` reads the capture as a number, which is right here:
# key sizes are ordered by how dangerous they are, so "smaller than 2048" is
# the question and a regex would be a list of the sizes anybody happens to use.

["val $KEY = $G.getInstance(\"RSA\")\n$$$_\n$KEY.initialize($BITS)",
 "val $KEY: $T = $G.getInstance(\"RSA\")\n$$$_\n$KEY.initialize($BITS)",
 "var $KEY = $G.getInstance(\"RSA\")\n$$$_\n$KEY.initialize($BITS)",
 "$KEY = $G.getInstance(\"RSA\")\n$$$_\n$KEY.initialize($BITS)"] as $generated
| scan_ast("*.kt"; $generated) as $all
| ($all | of($generated) | where_capture_compare("$BITS < 2048") | focus("BITS"))
| finding("use-of-weak-rsa-key";
    "$BITS bits is below the 2048 NIST has recommended since 2015, and every "
    + "signature a factored key made is forgeable")
| report
